Implement Git-native writeups as lightweight, versioned pre-tickets.


Writeups are a parallel workflow for capturing rough notes, ideas, design sketches, and work candidates before they are ready to become formal tickets. They should feel lighter than tickets, support multi-user collaboration without edit conflicts, and provide a clean path to promote or link the resulting work to tickets.


Each writeup has:

- UUID, prefix-addressable like tickets.
- Title.
- Status: `open` or `closed`, defaulting to `open`.
- Authors list, containing every user who has contributed a version.
- Versions list, ordered append-only.
- Ticket links list, containing associated ticket UUIDs.

Each version is a markdown document with optional YAML frontmatter:

```markdown
---
author: user@example.com
date: 2026-05-12T13:40:00Z
parent: [list-id of starting point]
---

Markdown content goes here.
```

The append-only version list is the collaboration primitive: edits do not rewrite existing thinking. A user adds a new version with their updated notes, and the history shows how the idea evolved.


Use the existing git-meta key/value storage, not a separate ref or directory layout.

Proposed keys:

```text
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:title    = string
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:status   = "open" | "closed"
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:authors  = set/list of author ids
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:versions = ordered list of markdown version bodies
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:tickets  = set/list of linked ticket UUIDs
```

For reverse lookups from tickets, also maintain:

```text
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:writeups = set/list of linked writeup UUIDs
```

This keeps writeups in the normal TicGit metadata namespace and lets `ti sync` move writeup data without a special sync path.


Add a `ti writeup` command group:

- `ti writeup new --title "..."` creates a writeup UUID, sets title/status, records the creator in authors, and optionally captures the first version.
- `ti writeup list` lists open writeups by default.
- `ti writeup show <id>` shows writeup metadata and latest version.
- `ti writeup show <id> --all` shows full version history.
- `ti writeup edit <id>` opens an editor and appends a new version with current author/date frontmatter.
- `ti writeup promote <id>` creates a ticket from the latest version content and links the writeup to the created ticket.
- `ti writeup close <id>` marks the writeup closed.
- `ti writeup archive <id>` can be treated as a soft-close/archive action if separate archive semantics are desired.
- `ti writeup link <writeup-id> <ticket-id>` creates writeup-to-ticket and ticket-to-writeup associations.
- `ti writeup unlink <writeup-id> <ticket-id>` removes both sides of that association.


- Prefix resolution should work for writeup ids the same way ticket id prefixes do.
- `list` should default to open writeups and have a way to include closed/all writeups.
- `show` should present title, status, authors, linked tickets, latest version, and enough version metadata to understand provenance.
- `edit` should append a version rather than mutating prior versions.
- `authors` should be updated when a new version is appended by someone not already in the list.
- `promote` should create at least one ticket from the latest version; start with single-ticket promotion unless there is already an established UI pattern for splitting.
- `link`/`unlink` should keep both forward and reverse metadata in sync.


- Whether writeups need tags immediately, or whether linked tickets and title search are enough for the first version.
- Whether `promote` should eventually support extracting multiple tickets from one writeup.
- Whether comments on writeups are needed, or whether append-only versions are sufficient.
- Whether `archive` should be distinct from `close` or simply an alias/state.